{"tool_name":"patchdock","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"patchdock","home":"http://bioinfo3d.cs.tau.ac.il/PatchDock/","summary":"PatchDock is an algorithm for molecular docking. The input is two molecules of any type: proteins, DNA, peptides, drugs. The output is a list of potential complexes sorted by shape complementarity criteria.","addition_date":"2017-03-24T11:31:23Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T13:48:48.082387Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Web application"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:patchdock","credit":[{"email":"duhovka@gmail.com","name":"PatchDock Administrator","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://bioinfo3d.cs.tau.ac.il/PatchDock/help.html"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["Afrith","geet","shofiqul"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Molecular docking","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0478"},{"term":"Protein binding site prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2575"},{"term":"Protein structure comparison","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2487"},{"term":"Protein structure prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0474"},{"term":"Protein-ligand docking","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0482"}]}],"name":"PatchDock","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"lupeilong","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkh413","metadata":{"abstract":"Here, we describe BioInfo3D, a suite of freely available web services for protein structural analysis. The FlexProt method performs flexible structural alignment of protein molecules. FlexProt simultaneously detects the hinge regions and aligns the rigid subparts of the molecules. It does not require an a priori knowledge of the flexible hinge regions. MultiProt and MASS perform simultaneous comparison of multiple protein structures. PatchDock performs prediction of protein-protein and protein-small molecule interactions. The input to all services is either protein PDB codes or protein structures uploaded to the server. All the services are available at http://bioinfo3d.cs.tau.ac.il. \u00a9 Oxford University Press 2004; all rights reserved.","authors":[{"name":"Dror O."},{"name":"Nussinov R."},{"name":"Schneidman-Duhovny D."},{"name":"Shatsky M."},{"name":"Wolfson H.J."}],"citationCount":24,"date":"2004-07-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"BioInfo3D: A suite of tools for structural bioinformatics"},"pmcid":"PMC441551","pmid":"15215437"},{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gki481","metadata":{"abstract":"Here, we describe two freely available web servers for molecular docking. The PatchDock method performs structure prediction of protein-protein and protein-small molecule complexes. The SymmDock method predicts the structure of a homomultimer with cyclic symmetry given the structure of the monomeric unit. The inputs to the servers are either protein PDB codes or uploaded protein structures. The services are available at http://bioinfo3d.cs.tau.ac.il. The methods behind the servers are very efficient, allowing large-scale docking experiments. \u00a9 2005 Oxford University Press.","authors":[{"name":"Inbar Y."},{"name":"Nussinov R."},{"name":"Schneidman-Duhovny D."},{"name":"Wolfson H.J."}],"citationCount":2518,"date":"2005-07-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"PatchDock and SymmDock: Servers for rigid and symmetric docking"},"pmcid":"PMC1160241","pmid":"15980490"},{"doi":"10.1002/prot.10397","metadata":{"abstract":"We present a very efficient rigid \"unbound\" soft docking methodology, which is based on detection of geometric shape complementarity, allowing liberal steric clash at the interface. The method is based on local shape feature matching, avoiding the exhaustive search of the 6D transformation space. Our experiments at CAPRI rounds 1 and 2 show that although the method does not perform an exhaustive search of the 6D transformation space, the \"correct\" solution is never lost. However, such a solution might rank low for large proteins, because there are alternatives with significantly larger geometrically compatible interfaces. In many cases this problem can be resolved by successful a priori focusing on the vicinity of potential binding sites as well as the extension of the technique to flexible (hinge-bent) docking. This is demonstrated in the experiments performed as a lesson from our CAPRI experience. \u00a9 2003 Wiley-Liss, Inc.","authors":[{"name":"Barzilai A."},{"name":"Benyamini H."},{"name":"Dror O."},{"name":"Halperin I."},{"name":"Haspel N."},{"name":"Inbar Y."},{"name":"Nussinov R."},{"name":"Polak V."},{"name":"Schneidman-Duhovny D."},{"name":"Shatsky M."},{"name":"Wolfson H.J."}],"citationCount":228,"date":"2003-07-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Proteins: Structure, Function and Genetics","title":"Taking geometry to its edge: Fast unbound rigid (and hinge-bent) docking"},"pmid":"12784375"}],"topic":[{"term":"Molecular modelling","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_2275"},{"term":"Protein structural motifs and surfaces","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0166"},{"term":"Small molecules","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0154"},{"term":"Structure analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0081"},{"term":"Structure prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0082"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"PatchDock","home":"https://bio.tools/patchdock","summary":"PatchDock is an algorithm for molecular docking. The input is two molecules of any type: proteins, DNA, peptides, drugs. The output is a list of potential complexes sorted by shape complementarity criteria.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:patchdock/cmd/bioinfo3d.cs.tau.ac.il","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"general":true},"